;;; `std.algebra/surjective-owed` — the requirement, outstanding, **from the
;;; other side**.
;;;
;;; `23_total_owed.ein`'s dual, and the pair is what makes the direction a
;;; test rather than a coincidence. Both rules assert the same atom — `(open
;;; ?R)`, the relation and nothing else — and the direction is not written
;;; anywhere in the head: it falls out of **which slot the rule's own `absent`
;;; leaves free**. `total-owed` scans `?b` and owes each `a` an image;
;;; `surjective-owed` scans `?a` and owes each `b` a preimage. No position
;;; index, no numeral, which is what the numeral-free decision demanded and
;;; what the superseded `(open ?R 0 ?a)` violated
;;; (plans/m1d_satisfiability/p1d.2_obligations/obligation_forms.md § G).
;;;
;;; So a mutant that swapped the two scans would be invisible in `23` and is
;;; caught here: the count would move from Soup to Ann.
;;;
;;; ── What this program does to the rule ─────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two people, two foods, one arrow — the same shape as `23`, read the other
;;; way:
;;;
;;;   (likes Bob Stew)     Stew has a fan; its requirement is discharged
;;;   Soup                 nobody likes it — one debt
;;;
;;; One instance, and it names Soup rather than Ann, which is exactly the
;;; difference from `23`. `total-owed` is **not** imported: separation by
;;; activation, `05_no_room.ein`'s rule.
;;;
;;; `26_surjective_owed_satisfied.ein` adds the arrow that pays it.
;;;
;;; The owe claim is asserted in `ein-infer/tests/obligation_reports.rs` —
;;; `:expect` speaks only of facts, and an `open` conclusion is never one.

(import std.algebra :symbols (surjective-owed))

(relation is-a  T T         :why "{?1} is a {?2}")
(relation likes Person Food :why "{?1} likes {?2}")

(is-a Ann Person) (is-a Bob Person)
(is-a Soup Food)  (is-a Stew Food)

(surjective-owed likes is-a)

(likes Bob Stew)

(query
  :goal (likes ?who ?what)
  :no-hypothesis (is-a likes)
  :expect (model (likes Bob Stew)))
